# ── WEIGHTS AND RUN ARTIFACTS ────────────────────────────────────────────
# ⛔ Each adapter_model.safetensors is 308 MB and every checkpoint carries an
# optimizer.pt beside it. GitHub HARD-REJECTS any file over 100 MB, and a large
# blob committed even once stays in the history forever — the repair is a history
# rewrite, so this file has to exist before the first commit, not after.
#
# Nothing of scientific value is lost: the ledger, the logs and the measured
# numbers ARE committed (runs/act2/*/logs, *.jsonl below), and the adapters are
# reproducible from the corpus builder plus the pinned training command in
# docs/RUNBOOK_ACT2_PILOT.md.
runs/act2/tlon_run*/adapter/
runs/act2/adapter/
**/checkpoint-*/
*.safetensors
*.bin
optimizer.pt
scheduler.pt

# ── THE PUZZLE'S BENCH ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ⛔⛔ THIS IS OTHER PEOPLE'S CONVERSATIONS. The puzzle is a public, anonymous
# app and `puzzle/data/*.sqlite3` is what strangers typed into it. It is not an
# artifact, it has no scientific value, and committing it would publish it —
# the repo is MIT and deliberately open, which makes this the one directory
# here where "publish everything" is the wrong default.
puzzle/data/

# ⛔⛔⛔ AND THE SNAPSHOT, WHEREVER IT LANDS. `/admin/snapshot` writes a
# settled copy of the log and the operator pulls it home with `modal volume
# get` -- so the one file in this repo that holds EVERY reader's conversation
# arrives as a download, into whatever directory the operator happened to be
# standing in. `puzzle/data/` above does not cover the repo root.
# ⭐ A belt-and-braces rule, because the cost of the miss is publishing
# strangers' words to a public MIT repository and it cannot be taken back.
log-snapshot.sqlite3
log.sqlite3
*.sqlite3-wal
*.sqlite3-shm

# ── THE CORPUS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ⭐ 80,000 rows / ~36 MB, and FULLY REGENERABLE:
#     python tools/act2_build_corpus.py --n 40000
# Deterministic at seed 20620 against the frozen lexicon, so the builder plus the
# recorded sha256 is a stronger artifact than the file — anyone can rebuild it
# and check they got the identical bytes.
runs/act2/corpus/*.jsonl
# same convention for the multi-turn corpus: the rows are
# reproducible from act2_build_multiturn.py + the manifest,
# which IS committed (it carries the provocation sha, the
# force map, the design zeros, and the held-variable record).
runs/act2/corpus_mt/*.jsonl
# and the ki-target probe's two corpora — 27 MB each, seed-pinned and
# sha-pinned in tools/pipeline_ki_target.sh, so they rebuild byte-identical.
runs/act2/ki_target/corpus_*/*.jsonl
# same for the recipe-variance seeds — sha-pinned in the pipeline
runs/act2/recipe_var/corpus_*/*.jsonl

# ── PYTHON ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.venv/
venv/
*.egg-info/

# ── LOCAL / EDITOR ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp

# ⛔⛔ THE STEERED PUZZLE CORPUS IS **NOT** IGNORED, AND THE REASON IS THE
# REASON THE OTHERS ARE. Every exclusion above is justified by REGENERABILITY —
# "deterministic at seed 20620 ... anyone can rebuild it and check they got the
# identical bytes", which makes the builder plus a sha a stronger artifact than
# the file. `runs/act2/corpus_conv_steered/` breaks that premise: it came from a
# hosted model at temperature 1.0 and cost $62.97, so no seed reproduces it and
# the builder is NOT a substitute for the bytes. It is 3.6 MB, it is the only
# copy, and its 97.1% root-carry claim is only auditable if the rows are here.
# ⭐ So it is committed on purpose. Do not "tidy" it into this file.

# ── KEPT ON PURPOSE (documenting the exceptions, so they are not "missed") ──
# !runs/act2/**/logs/          the measured output of every run
# !runs/act2/**/ledger.jsonl   the spend + result ledger
# !runs/act2/corpus/meta.json  the exposure report the corpus was built to

# ── COST LEDGERS — NOT PUBLIC ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Spend accounting is Nate's, not the research record. The ledgers stay on the
# machine; nothing about a result depends on what it cost.
**/ledger_tlon.jsonl
**/ledger_harden.jsonl
runs/act2/ledger.jsonl

# ⭐ THE PUZZLE'S TRAINING ROWS ARE REGENERABLE AND THEREFORE NOT COMMITTED —
# the same test every exclusion above passes. `corpus_bench_steered/` is an
# exact function of the three committed inputs at seed 20624, verified
# byte-identical on a clean rebuild, and `pipeline_puzzle.sh` rebuilds it ON THE
# BOX and refuses unless its sha matches. Committing it would put a 9.4 MB copy
# in the repo that can go stale against its own inputs without anything saying
# so — which is worse than not having it, because it looks authoritative.
runs/act2/corpus_bench_steered/
runs/act2/corpus_bench/
runs/act2/corpus_bench_rowmatch/
runs/act2/corpus_bench_dosed/
runs/act2/corpus_bench_dose75/
# ⭐ v2, same test: an exact function of `corpus_conv_force` +
# `corpus_conv_force_unsteered` (both committed) at dose 0.50, seed 20624.
runs/act2/corpus_bench_force/

# ⛔ Wrangler's local build cache. It appeared the first time the Worker was
# deployed and is pure machine state — a rebuilt bundle, a dev-server sqlite
# file and per-machine paths. Committing it to a PUBLIC repo would put local
# absolute paths in the history and create a file that conflicts on every
# deploy from a different machine.
puzzle/worker/.wrangler/
